-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 1: The Grand Paradox — All Power and No Power
--
-- § 3:  Executive power is with the King.
-- § 12: The King has supreme authority.
-- § 14: The King can do NOTHING without a minister's signature.
-- § 15: Ministers serve at Folketing's pleasure.
--
-- Therefore: the person who constitutionally holds ALL the power
-- can exercise NONE of it independently.
--
-- This is not a bug. It's the most elegant constitutional design
-- possible: separate the HOLDER of power from the WIELDER of power,
-- making the wielder accountable and the holder incapable of tyranny.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 1: The Grand Paradox — All Power and No Power ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 3:  'Den udovende magt er hos Kongen'")
@ print("  § 12: 'den hoejeste myndighed over alle rigets anliggender'")
@ print("  § 14: Nothing valid without minister's countersignature")
@ print("  § 15: Ministers serve at Folketing's pleasure")
@ print("")
@ print("  The King holds ALL power (§§ 3, 12)")
@ print("  The King can exercise NONE of it (§ 14)")
@ print("  This is constitutional DESIGN, not a flaw:")
@ print("  Separate the holder from the wielder.")
@ print("  The holder cannot be tyrant (no action without minister).")
@ print("  The wielder can be removed (no confidence vote).")
@ print("")
@ print("  In Futuruna terms: the type says Hos(Kongen) for executive,")
@ print("  but the TRANSITION RULES require countersignature.")
@ print("  The TYPE is a lie. The RULES tell the truth.")
@ print("  This is a constitutional STATE vs TRANSITION gap —")
@ print("  the same structural pattern as the Muslim King paradox.")
@ print("")

-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 2: The Accountability Inversion
--
-- The person WITH power has NO responsibility.
-- The person WITH responsibility has NO independent power.
--
-- § 13: King is ansvarsfri (irresponsible) + fredhellig (sacrosanct)
-- § 14: Minister signs → minister is responsible
--
-- The minister who signs a bad decision goes to Rigsretten (§ 16).
-- The King who ordered it cannot be touched (§ 13).
--
-- This is not injustice — it's a forcing function:
-- Ministers will refuse to sign decisions that would get them convicted.
-- The King's irresponsibility makes ministers MORE careful, not less.
-- ============================================================================

> ansvars_placering(er_kongen: Bool) -> Ansvarstype {
    if er_kongen { AnsvarsFri }
    else { Ansvarlig }
}

> magt_placering(er_kongen: Bool) -> String {
    if er_kongen { "Formel — hoejeste myndighed" }
    else { "Reel — kontrasignatur giver retsvirkning" }
}

@ print("--- INSIGHT 2: The Accountability Inversion ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  Kongen: magt = " + magt_placering(True))
@ print("  Kongen: ansvar = " + show(ansvars_placering(True)))
@ print("  Minister: magt = " + magt_placering(False))
@ print("  Minister: ansvar = " + show(ansvars_placering(False)))
@ print("")
@ print("  Power and responsibility are INVERSELY distributed.")
@ print("  This is a forcing function: ministers refuse bad decisions")
@ print("  because THEY go to Rigsretten (§ 16), not the King.")
@ print("  The King's irresponsibility makes ministers MORE careful.")
@ print("")

-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 3: The Fredhellig Paradox — Irresistible Force vs Immovable Object
--
-- § 13: The King is fredhellig (sacrosanct — cannot be prosecuted)
-- § 34: Attacking Folketing is hoejforraederi (high treason)
--
-- What if the King attacks Folketing?
-- § 34 says: the attacker commits high treason
-- § 13 says: the King cannot be held accountable
--
-- Both apply simultaneously. The constitution has no resolution.
--
-- This is not hypothetical — it's the scenario that § 34 was
-- specifically designed to prevent (protecting Parliament from
-- the King who commands the military per § 12).
-- ============================================================================

> kongen_angriber_folketing_paragraf_13() -> String {
    "Kongen er fredhellig — kan ikke retsforfølges"
}

> kongen_angriber_folketing_paragraf_34() -> String {
    "Angreb på Folketinget er højforræderi"
}

@ print("--- INSIGHT 3: The Fredhellig Paradox ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  Scenario: The King attacks Folketing")
@ print("  § 13 says: " + kongen_angriber_folketing_paragraf_13())
@ print("  § 34 says: " + kongen_angriber_folketing_paragraf_34())
@ print("")
@ print("  Both apply. No resolution mechanism.")
@ print("  The person § 34 was designed to constrain (the commander")
@ print("  of military forces, § 12) is the same person § 13 makes")
@ print("  unprosecutable.")
@ print("")
@ print("  Resolution paths:")
@ print("  1. Ministers refuse to sign military orders (§ 14)")
@ print("     → but the King might bypass this")
@ print("  2. Soldiers refuse orders (§ 34 — obedience is no defense)")
@ print("     → but the King is their supreme commander (§ 12)")
@ print("  3. § 9 delegation: law determines regency for 'other reasons'")
@ print("     → but who triggers this without a functioning Folketing?")
@ print("")
@ print("  This is the DEEPEST unresolvable paradox in §§ 1-34.")
@ print("")

-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 4: The Emergency Decree Perverse Incentive
--
-- § 23: Emergency decrees when Folketing CANNOT assemble
-- § 34: Preventing Folketing from assembling = high treason
--
-- The perverse incentive:
--   If you commit high treason (§ 34) by preventing assembly,
--   you CREATE the conditions for emergency decrees (§ 23).
--   The government can then rule by decree — legally.
--
-- The decrees are legal (§ 23 conditions met).
-- The situation that created them is illegal (§ 34).
-- But the legality of the decrees doesn't depend on HOW
-- the situation arose — only that Folketing can't assemble.
-- ============================================================================

> folketing_kan_samles(forhindret: Bool) -> Bool {
    if forhindret { False } else { True }
}

> kan_udstede_provisorisk_lov(forhindret: Bool) -> Bool {
    if forhindret { True } else { False }
}

> forhindring_er_hoejforraederi(forhindret_af_person: Bool) -> Bool {
    if forhindret_af_person { True } else { False }
}

@ print("--- INSIGHT 4: The Emergency Decree Perverse Incentive ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 34: Preventing Folketing from assembling = hoejforraederi")
@ print("  § 23: If Folketing cannot assemble → emergency decrees allowed")
@ print("")
= forhindret = True
@ print("  Folketing forhindret: " + show(folketing_kan_samles(forhindret)))
@ print("  Kan udstede noed-love: " + show(kan_udstede_provisorisk_lov(forhindret)))
@ print("  Er forhindringen hoejforraederi: " + show(forhindring_er_hoejforraederi(forhindret)))
@ print("")
@ print("  The decrees are LEGAL (§ 23 conditions met).")
@ print("  The situation is ILLEGAL (§ 34 violated).")
@ print("  But § 23 doesn't check HOW the situation arose.")
@ print("  Perverse incentive: treason CREATES legal emergency powers.")
@ print("")
@ print("  Fix: § 23 should require that the inability to assemble")
@ print("  is not caused by unconstitutional action.")
@ print("  Futuruna: > provisorisk_lov_gyldig() -> Bool")
@ print("           under ikke_forhindret_ulovligt()")
@ print("")

-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 5: The Self-Sealing Exclusion
--
-- § 30: Eligibility requires "not convicted of a disqualifying act"
--        judged by "almindeligt omdoemme" (general public judgment)
-- § 33: Folketing judges its own members' eligibility
--
-- Who defines "almindeligt omdoemme"? The law, set by... Folketing.
-- Who applies it? Folketing (§ 33).
-- Who reviews the decision? Nobody. No court can overrule (§ 33).
--
-- Folketing defines the standard, applies the standard, and is
-- the final judge of the outcome. This is a CLOSED LOOP.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 5: The Self-Sealing Exclusion ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 30: Ineligible if convicted of 'uvaerdig' act")
@ print("  § 30: 'uvaerdig' defined by 'almindeligt omdoemme'")
@ print("  § 33: Folketing judges eligibility. No external review.")
@ print("")
@ print("  Who defines the standard? Folketing (through law)")
@ print("  Who applies the standard? Folketing (§ 33)")
@ print("  Who reviews the decision? Nobody (§ 33 — sole judge)")
@ print("")
@ print("  This is a CLOSED LOOP — Folketing can exclude anyone")
@ print("  by defining 'uvaerdig' to include them, then judging")
@ print("  them by its own standard, with no appeal.")
@ print("")
@ print("  Safeguard: new elections. Excluded member can run again")
@ print("  if re-elected — but Folketing can exclude again (§ 33).")
@ print("  The only real check is the voters themselves.")
@ print("")

-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 6: The Non-Lutheran Parliament Scenario
--
-- § 6:  King must be Lutheran
-- § 29: MPs need citizenship + residence + age + capacity (no religion!)
-- § 14: Ministers countersign for the King
-- § 15: Ministers serve at Folketing's pleasure
--
-- There is NO religious requirement for MPs or ministers.
-- A fully non-Lutheran Folketing is constitutionally possible.
-- Such a Folketing appoints non-Lutheran ministers.
-- Those ministers countersign for the Lutheran King.
--
-- The state church constrains the HEAD OF STATE but not the
-- ACTUAL GOVERNMENT. The religion requirement is purely ceremonial.
-- ============================================================================

> religion_krav_for_institution(inst: Institution) -> Bool {
    match inst {
        | Kongen -> True
        | _ -> False
    }
}

@ print("--- INSIGHT 6: The Non-Lutheran Parliament Scenario ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  Religious requirement by institution:")
@ print("    Kongen: " + show(religion_krav_for_institution(Kongen)))
@ print("    Folketinget: " + show(religion_krav_for_institution(Folketinget)))
@ print("    Domstolene: " + show(religion_krav_for_institution(Domstolene)))
@ print("    Ministerraadet: " + show(religion_krav_for_institution(Ministerraadet)))
@ print("")
@ print("  A fully non-Lutheran Folketing is constitutional.")
@ print("  Non-Lutheran ministers governing through a Lutheran King.")
@ print("  The religion requirement constrains the figurehead,")
@ print("  not the actual government.")
@ print("  § 6 is constitutionally ceremonial.")
@ print("")

-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 7: The Dissolution Infinite Loop
--
-- § 15: No confidence → PM resigns or calls election
-- § 32: King can dissolve at any time (through PM per § 12)
--
-- Scenario:
--   1. Folketing votes no confidence
--   2. PM calls election (§ 15)
--   3. New Folketing immediately votes no confidence
--   4. PM calls election again
--   5. Repeat forever
--
-- No circuit breaker exists in §§ 1-34.
-- The German Grundgesetz has a constructive vote of no confidence
-- (must elect a replacement). The Danish constitution does not.
-- ============================================================================

@ print("--- INSIGHT 7: The Dissolution Infinite Loop ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  1. Folketing votes no confidence (§ 15)")
@ print("  2. PM calls election (§ 15 stk. 2)")
@ print("  3. New Folketing votes no confidence again")
@ print("  4. PM calls election again (§ 32)")
@ print("  5. No constitutional circuit breaker")
@ print("")
@ print("  German Grundgesetz: constructive vote (must elect replacement)")
@ print("  Danish Grundlov: destructive vote (no replacement required)")
@ print("  The Danish system assumes political culture will prevent loops.")
@ print("  The constitution provides NO mechanism to break the cycle.")
@ print("")

-- ============================================================================
-- INSIGHT 8: The Oath Web — Who Swears to Whom?
--
-- § 8:  King swears to uphold the constitution (Statsraadet)
-- § 27: Royal appointees swear an oath (forsikring)
-- § 32: MPs swear to uphold the constitution
--
-- Three oath-takers, all swearing to the same document:
--   The King swears to the constitution
--   The civil servants swear to the constitution
--   The MPs swear to the constitution
--
-- But nobody swears to EACH OTHER. Every oath goes to the
-- document, not to a person or institution. The constitution
-- is the ONLY point of mutual commitment.
--
-- Remove the constitution and there is no oath web. No loyalty.
-- The document is not just the rules — it's the SOCIAL CONTRACT.
-- ============================================================================

# EdAflaegger = Kongen_ | Tjenestemand_ | Folketingsmedlem_

> ed_aflaegges_til() -> String {
    "Grundloven"
}

@ print("--- INSIGHT 8: The Oath Web ---")
@ print("")
@ print("  § 8:  Kongen svaerger til Grundloven")
@ print("  § 27: Kongelige embedsmaend svaerger til Grundloven")
@ print("  § 32: Folketingsmedlemmer svaerger til Grundloven")
@ print("")
@ print("  Nobody swears to each other. Every oath goes to the DOCUMENT.")
@ print("  The constitution is not just rules — it's the sole point of")
@ print("  mutual commitment. Without it: no oath web, no loyalty.")
@ print("  The King doesn't swear to serve Parliament.")
@ print("  Parliament doesn't swear to serve the King.")
@ print("  Both swear to serve the constitution.")
@ print("  The document IS the social contract. Literally.")
@ print("")

